# Wakelet

> Wakelet is an all-in-one content curation and collaboration platform. It lets teachers, students, schools, districts, universities, teams, and individuals save links, files, videos, images, and notes into visual collections, then organize, collaborate on, and share them through a single link. Wakelet's primary focus is education, with plans built for classrooms, departments, schools, districts, and higher education institutions.

Key facts about Wakelet:

- Company: Wakelet Technologies Limited, headquartered in Manchester, UK, with operations in the US. Founded by Jamil Khalil (Founder and CEO).
- Reach: used by millions of people across 170 countries, with more than 25 million collections created. Organizations named as users include Harvard University, the NHS, the World Health Organization, and the UK Government, alongside US school districts.
- Safety and compliance: COPPA, FERPA, CSPC, New York Education Law 2-d, and GDPR compliant. Meets WCAG 2.1 AA accessibility standards. Collections are private by default and nothing is public until the owner shares it.
- Core capabilities: collections of links, files, videos, images, and notes; collaborative collections that people can join by link or join code without an account; student digital portfolios; activities and assignments for students; newsletters and resource hubs; layouts and design options; Wakelet AI; sharing to Google Classroom and Microsoft Teams; integrations including Canva and Adobe Express.
- Education administration: account rostering via Google, Microsoft, Clever, ClassLink, or CSV upload; multi-level organization for districts, schools, departments, and classrooms; students can retain portfolios after graduation on Education Enterprise. Purchase Orders are accepted for eligible school, district, and organization purchases.
- Plans: Starter (free), Pro, Education Group, Education Enterprise, Group, and Enterprise. Current pricing and feature comparison are on the pricing page linked below.
- Access: Wakelet runs as a web app in any modern browser on phones, tablets, laptops, and Chromebooks, with add-to-home-screen support on iOS and Android. A browser extension is available for Chrome and Microsoft Edge. There are no native app store apps.

## Education

- [Wakelet for Education](https://wakelet.com/site/education/for-education): Hub page for K-12 and higher education. Explains how teachers, students, departments, and schools use Wakelet to distribute resources, build portfolios, collaborate, and share learning, with an overview of education plans and compliance.

### By institution

- [Wakelet for Schools](https://wakelet.com/site/education/schools): Whole-school resource hubs, student portfolios across subjects and year groups, staff and family communication, and department collaboration. Covers school and district rollout.
- [Wakelet for Higher Education](https://wakelet.com/site/education/higher-education): Course resource hubs, visual reading lists, research collections, department hubs, campus newsletters, and student portfolios that stay with learners after graduation.
- [Wakelet for Education Teams](https://wakelet.com/site/education/teams): Shared spaces for departments, tech coaches, and professional learning networks to collect resources, plan together, and keep professional learning materials in one place.

### By role

- [Wakelet for Teachers](https://wakelet.com/site/education/teachers): Curate teaching resources, build classroom hubs, create and assign activities with progress tracking and feedback, share everything through one link, and support student portfolios. Collections can be copied and reused for new classes.
- [Wakelet for Students](https://wakelet.com/site/education/students): Build a digital portfolio, organize research, collaborate on group work, add reflections, and share one link with teachers, universities, mentors, or employers.

### By use case

- [Resource Sharing](https://wakelet.com/site/education/resource-sharing): Create classroom resource hubs where students find links, files, videos, instructions, and activities in one place, organized by topic, lesson, subject, or project.
- [Digital Portfolios](https://wakelet.com/site/education/portfolios): Student digital portfolios that collect work, reflections, achievements, and evidence of progress beyond test scores. Includes the three-step collect, organize, share workflow and a demo request path for schools and districts.
- [Lesson Plans](https://wakelet.com/site/education/lesson-plans): Build digital lesson plans that sequence resources, instructions, examples, and activities in one space, then share them as a graded Activity or a simple collection.
- [Newsletters](https://wakelet.com/site/education/newsletters): Create visual school and classroom newsletters from links, media, and announcements, shared through one permanent link with families, staff, and students.
- [Classroom Collaboration](https://wakelet.com/site/education/collaboration): Shared collections and class Workspaces where students and educators contribute ideas, resources, and media, then curate the result into evidence of learning. Positioned as an alternative to online bulletin board tools.
- [Research and Reading Lists](https://wakelet.com/site/education/research): Collect sources, organize notes by theme or question, build reading lists, and present research as a shareable visual collection.

### Portrait of a Graduate

- [What Is a Portrait of a Graduate?](https://wakelet.com/site/education/portrait-of-a-graduate): Reference guide to Portrait of a Graduate frameworks: definition, why states and districts adopt them, the competencies that recur across versions, the six New York State attributes, state examples (New York, North Carolina, Kentucky, Utah, Vermont), district examples, how to evidence the attributes with student work, what rubrics look like, how to create a portrait, implementation challenges, and links to primary sources. Explains how attribute-tagged Wakelet portfolios are used as the evidence layer.
- [New York State Portrait of a Graduate and NY Inspires](https://wakelet.com/blog/new-york-state-portrait-of-a-graduate): District implementation guide covering the six attributes, the NY Inspires timeline, what changes for diploma requirements, and what districts can do before state guidance arrives.
- [Portrait of a Graduate Rubric Examples](https://wakelet.com/blog/portrait-of-a-graduate-rubric-from-leading-districts): How rubrics turn a named attribute into observable criteria and performance levels, using North Carolina's statewide rubrics as the working pattern.
- [Student Portfolios K-12: The Artifact-Led Approach](https://wakelet.com/blog/student-portfolios-k-12-the-artifact-led-approach): What separates a portfolio from a pile of work, and what to collect at each grade band.
- [AASA and Battelle for Kids](https://wakelet.com/blog/aasa-and-battelle-for-kids): Background on the organizations behind the Portrait of a Graduate model and what changed when Battelle for Kids joined AASA in July 2025.

